  "summary_text": "Kyle, yesterday was mixed signals across the portfolio. Port Washington had a rough day\u2014down 26% in total revenue\u2014but this is almost entirely a capacity story: you ran 4 groomers at $461/groomer versus your 30-day baseline of $498/groomer, which means productivity per head was 7% soft. The real concern is retail: down 50% and add-on attach fell from 61% to 47%, signaling front-of-house discipline slipped. This isn't a traffic problem (WTD is down 21% appointments, but MTD is only 2% behind DOW-adjusted expectation, meaning calendar mix explains most of the month's shortfall). Hicksville, by contrast, crushed it\u2014up 48% total revenue with 11 grooms in the hopper and retail exploding up 349%, though groomer productivity is 19% below baseline at $331/groomer, meaning volume masks softer per-head economics. Hicksville is running hot on transactions but thin on groomer efficiency.\n\nThe pattern: both stores are scheduling lean next week (Port Washington 34 groomer-days vs its typical 4.4/day run rate, Hicksville only 14 groomer-days versus need). Port Washington shows a positive leading indicator\u2014the 12+ week visit gap is shrinking, suggesting retention recovery\u2014but Hicksville's is growing, a red flag for stealth churn you should address with management. Inventory-wise, both locations have the same critical miss: X-Small Treat Table is deeply negative and moving 34\u201342 units monthly; restock that immediately.\n\n**Capacity & coverage:** Port Washington has 4 groomers and 8.0 bather hours today; Hicksville has 2 groomers and zero bather hours; next 7 days Port has 34 groomer-days and Hicksville has 14 groomer-days\u2014address the scheduling cliff on both locations and the retail/upsell discipline at Port."
